WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.133
An approved file root is the trusted base
directory for a specific workflow.

2
00:00:04.213 --> 00:00:07.903
It answers a simple question: where is
this operation allowed to touch files?

3
00:00:07.983 --> 00:00:10.991
A download workflow may have one root,
upload staging another,

4
00:00:11.071 --> 00:00:14.874
reports another, temporary work another,
and user-specific storage another.

5
00:00:14.954 --> 00:00:17.168
Naming those roots makes the policy
concrete.

6
00:00:17.248 --> 00:00:20.484
A trusted root should come from
configuration, deployment policy,

7
00:00:20.564 --> 00:00:24.537
or trusted application state, not directly
from the user request being processed.

8
00:00:24.617 --> 00:00:26.774
The root is part of the application
boundary.

9
00:00:26.854 --> 00:00:29.863
User-controlled input may select a file
inside that boundary,

10
00:00:29.943 --> 00:00:32.667
but it should not be able to redefine the
boundary itself.

11
00:00:32.747 --> 00:00:35.926
Separate roots help keep workflows from
bleeding into each other.

12
00:00:36.006 --> 00:00:39.979
A report export should not share the same
write area as application configuration.

13
00:00:40.059 --> 00:00:43.863
Upload staging should not silently overlap
served downloads unless the design

14
00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:45.419
intentionally supports that.

15
00:00:45.499 --> 00:00:49.472
User or tenant data should be separated by
directory design and access checks so one

16
00:00:49.552 --> 00:00:52.674
caller's resource name cannot select
another caller's storage.

17
00:00:52.754 --> 00:00:54.741
Least privilege strengthens the design.

18
00:00:54.821 --> 00:00:58.624
The operating system account running the
process should have access only to the

19
00:00:58.704 --> 00:01:01.372
directories and operations required for
the workflow.

20
00:01:01.452 --> 00:01:03.041
If path logic contains a mistake,

21
00:01:03.121 --> 00:01:07.151
restrictive process permissions and
storage layout can reduce what the mistake can

22
00:01:07.231 --> 00:01:07.629
affect.

23
00:01:07.709 --> 00:01:11.115
Application checks and operating system
limits should work together.

24
00:01:11.195 --> 00:01:15.225
Approved root policy should be documented
closely enough for code review to compare

25
00:01:15.305 --> 00:01:16.724
intent with implementation.

26
00:01:16.804 --> 00:01:19.983
Reviewers should know which root a
function uses, who controls it,

27
00:01:20.063 --> 00:01:23.185
which operations are allowed there, and
what naming rules apply.

28
00:01:23.265 --> 00:01:27.011
Without that policy, review becomes a
debate about strings rather than a check

29
00:01:27.091 --> 00:01:28.340
against a clear boundary.
